A||||11||BellaFiero||Hi Everyone... just wanted to introduce myself.||||6||
Z||000000||BellaFiero||06-29-2000||02:09 PM||||Hey, hey. The name’s Jayse, I’m a 19 year old LAN Administrator out of Saint Louis Mo, and like most of you, I have fallen in love with Fieros. I've been reading this Bulletin board for a while, and now I'd like to add my own thoughts to it occasionally. So HI! I own only one, and that’s the way I like it. I’m too busy with one Fiero to worry about another. My car is not body-modded to the max, but it does have a few interesting customisations to it. First of all, the Fi-Air-O scoops. The twin scoops that go over the roof and down to the engine vents ( I never was a fan of the Indy pace scoop ). My car began it’s life as a 1988 Coupe, with air conditioning. And that’s about it &#61514;. Not really any other options. Oh, it has tilt wheel and cruise control. Four cylinder engine. Of course, I’ve got a stock exhaust for a V6 ( a little clamping never hurt anyone ). That quad pipe… that one muffler… oh, the humanity of it all. I’ve replaced the shifter with a polished aluminum Razzo shifter, color-matched to the dark red metallic of my new paint job. The rims are Z-racing style 42s. Only 14 inchers. Brand new Goodyear tires. New brakes. The spoiler I stripped off a dying SE model I found in the back of a used car lot. Took the front Aero style nose as well. Both of these found their way onto my little four banger. Twin racing stripes, like those from the Firebirds in the Need For Speed High Stakes game, found their way as well. Custom sail panels with a fiery red and silver Pegasus, with “Jason’s” on the driver’s side and “Bella Donna” on the passenger’s. Yes, the car’s name is Bella Donna. I’ve got black velvet seat covers for my mint seats ( and I’ll hope to keep them that way – hence the covers ). And an interior Street Glowz kit ( about a 12” neon bar that runs between the headrests on the back shelf and twin beams under the dash to illuminate the floorboard of both driver and passenger ). The back window is limo tinted, the side windows were 35 tint, but good ol’ Smokey recommended I take those off before the warnings I got became tickets. I’ve got a Cobra radar detector sitting on the front windshield, and a couple of custom cut decals. “If It’s a Race Issue, Take it to the Track” and “Powered By Pontiac” on the over-the-hood scoops. Converted a ram-air kit to work for my Fiero, but the effects are only slightly noticeable. Still, it looks like a demon from the front. Now, all of this stuff didn't look very nice at first - kind of sticking out, or the panels weren't lining up. But my father was a mechanic for the air force before they threw him behind a desk ( damn that Senior Master rank anyway ) and he has kept up the hobby by restoring muscle cars. And I've been helping him since I was a little pup. So I am the king of customization ( read that as - making the damn thing fit ). Sheet metal, bracing, all quite acceptable in the process of restoring  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> Now, the car is flawless. Everything is specially customized inside and out, everything looks fluid and like it was designed to be there. The car has a wicked stance, and I find the “Fiero” mudflaps from GM are a nice touch ( only on the rear wheels ) to the back view. Makes the tires look a little wider. I’ve got Fox logos across the rear window and the front ( I’ve got a Yamaha bike, as well, from 1979 that only has 3,000 miles on it and is in perfect condition. I’m restoring the exhaust right now ((read that as : replacing  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> )). Under the hood, I’ve got an engine with 145,000 miles on it but still runs strong ( absolutely no problems yet ). Had to replace the alternator, fuel pump, radiator, and heater core the first year I bought her. But hey – any muscle car will nickel and dime you to death. And yes, <BR>I do consider her a muscle car.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> You want to start talking horsepower and what not, you want throw torque and tire size and speed and drag racing etiquette around, I’ll smack you down with a very simple fact : nerves of steel and weight. That’s what I think racing is really about. I’ve seen Rice-up Civics take out Iroc-Zs, and vice versa. It’s the driver – how hard is he willing to push that engine, and how quick is he off the line. Red light racing ( street racing ) is fun, and I enjoy it. But too much, and that poor engine feels the strain. You’re taking miles of life off that engine with each burn out. It’s called abuse. It may be fun, but it’s lethal. I’ve killed 80’s Corvettes and Mustangs, but I don’t pretend to be a Lamborghini. I do pretend to have a pretty souped 4 cylinder, a car in excellent condition, that makes these poor, neglected 80’s cars that are on the road look like rust buckets.<BR>Well, I’ll have pictures up sometime this weekend when my site goes on-line. That’ll be pretty awesome. And yes, the engine is pretty stock, but there have been a few minor tweaks and adjustments. Remember – the faster you go, the more gas money it takes to get there. I love my four cylinder, and I don’t want a bigger engine. It would be nice, don’t get me wrong, but there are plenty of advantages to a four cylinder. Plenty of them. Let’s have a spark plug changing race  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><BR>||||reg||6
